# Allow input method in the password field
#
# True, deliberately, and not for the reason the option name suggests.  This is
# the workaround for the trigger-key failure in docs/ime-chrome-diagnosis.md /
# issue #14, and it is the ONLY thing measured to fix it.
#
# Mechanism: after the COSMIC lock screen goes away, cosmic-comp re-activates the
# input method for the newly focused surface but never re-sends content_type, so
# fcitx5 keeps purpose=password from the lock screen.  With this option False,
# Instance::inputMethod() then pins the input method to keyboard-<layout> and
# ignores isActive() entirely — so Ctrl+Space is consumed and nothing switches,
# until an unrelated focus event makes the client re-assert its content type.
# Setting this True removes that branch.
#
# Measured under a lock/unlock provocation: 0 of 4 trials and 0 of 27 presses
# failed, against 18 of 18 trials failing across five control arms (apt 5.1.7,
# nix 5.1.16, nix 5.1.19, absolute Activate/Deactivate keys, ShareInputState=No).
# The version arms are what rule out "just upgrade fcitx5".
#
# The cost, stated plainly: a genuine password field can now receive input from
# an active input method — including the lock screen.  ShowPreeditForPassword
# stays False so nothing is rendered, but if mozc happens to be active when a
# password prompt takes focus, keystrokes go into a conversion buffer instead of
# the field.  ActiveByDefault=False plus the state being dropped at lock makes
# that rare, not impossible.  Revert this line if the compositor bug is fixed.
AllowInputMethodForPassword=True
# Show preedit text when typing password
ShowPreeditForPassword=False
# Interval of saving user data in minutes
AutoSavePeriod=30

